---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Install from "../components/Install.astro";
import BenchmarkSummary from "../components/BenchmarkSummary.astro";
import ReorderCallout from "../components/ReorderCallout.astro";
import EvidenceLinks from "../components/EvidenceLinks.astro";
import { BLOB, href, REPO } from "../site";
---
<Base
  path="/alien-signals-comparison/"
  title="alien-signals vs Reflex Quant: Benchmark, Performance and Design"
  headline="alien-signals vs Reflex Quant: Benchmark and Design"
  description="alien-signals 3.2.1 vs Reflex Quant: CI benchmark on 18 scenarios, dependency reordering counters, API, failure semantics and size. An alternative, measured."
  faq={[
    ["Is Reflex Quant an alternative to alien-signals?", "Yes: both provide framework-agnostic signal, computed and effect. Quant is smaller in surface, reuses dependency relations on reorder, and is specified by a written contract."],
    ["Is Reflex Quant faster than alien-signals?", "On the project's own 18-scenario CI suite its geometric-mean time is 0.45 of alien-signals' (CI run 42794143933). The suite is the project's own; per-scenario numbers are on the benchmarks page of this site."],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">Two small, framework-agnostic signals runtimes. One is known for raw speed. The other was derived from a written contract and then measured. They agree on the API and differ in what they consider part of the job.</p>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/stackblitz/alien-signals">alien-signals</a> is a fast, widely used signals library. Reflex Quant treats it the way its <a href={`${BLOB}/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>architecture rules</a> treat every other runtime: as a source of counterexamples and workloads, not of obligations. It is benchmarked against alien-signals 3.2.1 on every CI run, and before any timing both must produce the same answers on every scenario.</p>

  <h2>At a glance</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th></th><th>Reflex Quant</th><th>alien-signals 3.2.1</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Reactive primitives</td><td><code>signal</code>, <code>computed</code>, <code>effect</code></td><td><code>signal</code>, <code>computed</code>, <code>effect</code>, <code>effectScope</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reading and writing</td><td>functions over handles: <code>get</code>, <code>peek</code>, <code>set</code>, <code>update</code></td><td>the signal is a function: call it to read, call it with a value to write</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Previous value in a computed</td><td><code>computed((prev) =&gt; …, initial)</code>: the last committed value</td><td>see its documentation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Manual invalidation</td><td>none</td><td><code>trigger</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reordered reads (256 sources)</td><td>0 relations created or removed per pass</td><td>255 relinked per pass</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Depth of a chain</td><td>bounded by memory: one explicit work stack, no recursion</td><td>see its documentation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Specified by</td><td>a written contract, an executable spec, differential tests</td><td>its test suite</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Size (ESM, gzip)</td><td>about 2.2 KB</td><td>see <a href="https://bundlephobia.com/package/alien-signals">bundlephobia</a></td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>The structural difference: a reorder</h2>
  <p>When a computation reads the same sources in a different order, something has to happen to its dependency list. alien-signals unlinks and relinks on every out-of-place read. Quant keeps the relation and moves it.</p>
  <ReorderCallout />
  <p>This is counted, not timed, so it does not depend on a machine. It is also where the timing gap is widest: on the three reorder scenarios Quant takes 0.15 to 0.17 of alien-signals' time, and on a shared graph whose read order changes every pass, 0.22. How the old relation is found without a hash table: <a href={href("/dynamic-dependency-tracking/")}>dynamic dependency tracking</a>.</p>

  <h2>Writes inside an effect: two findings</h2>
  <p>The <a href={href("/fireflies/")}>Fireflies</a> model was ported to six runtimes on a fixed 64 × 40 grid and checked against a hand-written worklist after every tick. It is a hard model for a reactive runtime: a firefly that flashes writes into its neighbours' inboxes from inside its own transition, and some of those writes land on a computation that is still running. The code, the ports and the commands are in <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/bench/fireflies`}>bench/fireflies</a>.</p>
  <aside class="callout warn" role="note" aria-label="Warning">
    <div class="callout-head">Warning: a wake-up can be lost in alien-signals 3.2.1</div>
    <p>If an effect's dependency is written while that effect is still running, by another effect that ran inside one of its own writes, the effect is not run again. The new value stays unread until something else wakes it.</p>
    <p>You are exposed if your effects write signals that other effects read, and those effects write back. Plain derived values and effects that only read are not affected.</p>
    <p>Reproduce in a few lines: <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/bench/fireflies/lost-wakeup.mjs`}>lost-wakeup.mjs</a>. Observed on 3.2.1 only; other versions were not tested, and this may be intended behaviour.</p>
  </aside>
  <p><strong>A wake-up is lost.</strong> Quant, Preact Signals, Vue, Solid and Angular's signal primitives end every one of 1,500 ticks in the same state as the worklist. The direct alien-signals port differs on the first tick: fireflies are left with unread pulses. The case reduces to two effects. A writes into B's inbox; B's effect runs inside that write and writes back into A's inbox while A is still running.</p>
  <table class="num">
    <tr><th></th><th>A received</th><th>left unread in A's inbox</th></tr>
    <tr><td>alien-signals 3.2.1</td><td>0</td><td>0.2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Preact Signals 1.14.5</td><td>0.2</td><td>0</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Reflex Quant 1.1.2</strong></td><td><strong>0.2</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>In alien-signals the effect is not run again for a dependency that was written during its own run. In Quant this exact case is an invariant of the contract (W2: a watcher invalidated during its own run is delivered again). Whether alien-signals intends the behaviour is a question for its maintainers; the test only shows what it does. To get the right answer the port needs a repair written by hand: after every tick, re-trigger each firefly that still has mail, until none does.</p>
  <p><strong>An avalanche lives on the call stack.</strong> In alien-signals a write inside an effect runs the dependent effect inside that write, so a cascade nests effects in one another. On this model the nesting reaches 2,560, the whole field. On Node's default stack the repaired port overflowed at tick 262 with 2,457 effects nested in one run and completed in another: at this size it sits on the limit. Quant runs propagation on its own work stack, and its depth here is bounded by memory.</p>
  <p>The same ports on a 200 × 200 field, 40,000 fireflies, 900 ticks, default stack size:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th></th><th>result</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Reflex Quant</strong></td><td>same state as the worklist; all 40,000 flash in one tick; 5.3 s</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Angular signal primitives</td><td>same state as the worklist; 7.6 s</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Solid</td><td>same state as the worklist; 44.7 s</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Preact Signals</td><td><code>Cycle detected</code> at tick 167, its limit on repeated effect runs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Vue reactivity</td><td>call stack overflow at tick 341</td></tr>
    <tr><td>alien-signals, repaired port</td><td>call stack overflow at tick 247</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="note">One model, one machine (Node 25.2.0), each port one reasonable way to write it. This is a probe of semantics and stack behaviour, not a ranking; the timings are in the folder's README.</p>

  <h2>Where alien-signals is faster</h2>
  <p>On the shortest paths. Reading a clean value (<code>static.read-clean</code>) costs Quant 1.22 of alien-signals' time, and mounting effects (<code>lifecycle.mount-100</code>) 1.04. A read that finds nothing to do is almost all call overhead, and alien-signals has less of it. The pattern across the suite is consistent: the more a scenario looks like a graph, with shared nodes, fan-in and changing read order, the further Quant pulls ahead; the more it looks like a single function call, the closer alien-signals gets, and on the two above it wins.</p>

  <h2>Benchmarks</h2>
  <BenchmarkSummary />
  <p>Per-scenario chart and method: <a href={href("/benchmarks/")}>benchmarks</a>.</p>

  <h2>What Quant leaves out on purpose</h2>
  <p><code>effectScope</code>, nested-effect ownership and manual invalidation are real answers to real problems: who owns a group of effects, and how to tell the graph that a mutable container changed. They are answers to different questions from the one Quant asks. Its rule for admitting a mechanism is a named obligation and a minimal program that violates it without the mechanism. "Another runtime has it" is explicitly not a reason. So Quant has <code>dispose</code> for one node and no notion of a scope; ownership is left to the framework above it.</p>
  <p>That is a cost if you want those features in the box, and the reason the runtime is small if you do not.</p>

  <h2>What Quant writes down</h2>
  <p>What a computed depends on after it throws. Whether A → B → A is a change. What happens to a computation whose source is written while it runs. Whether an obligation to run survives a failure. Quant answers each of these in its <a href={`${BLOB}/CONTRACT.md`}>contract</a>, executes the answers against a specification on over 140,000 programs, and removes each mechanism in turn to check that a test notices. This page makes no claim about how alien-signals behaves in those cases; only that Quant's behaviour is specified and tested there.</p>

  <h2>When to choose which</h2>
  <p>Choose alien-signals if you want effect scopes in the library, a large user base, and the fastest possible read of a clean value. Choose Reflex Quant if your graphs reorder and share, if you want behaviour under failure written down, or if you want a runtime small enough to read in an afternoon and argue with.</p>

  <h2>Try Quant</h2>
  <Install />
  <EvidenceLinks items={["bench", "report", "churn", "contract"]} />
</Base>
